{
  "image_name": "5ec55e8ec83ea6202d4152fd5b0db8fa.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic overview of how cellulose-based materials can be assembled or functionalized through several classes of noncovalent and coordination interactions. Across the full diagram, cellulose chains are shown as green strands that connect with molecules, ions, nanoparticles, or other polymers to create different structures and properties.\n\nA major theme is that hydrogen bonding is central to cellulose organization, enabling directional networks, twisting behavior, solvent-controlled assembly, and dense crosslinking in nanocomposites. Electrostatic interactions provide another route, through ion pairing, stabilization of liquid–liquid interfaces such as Pickering emulsions, and charged nanotemplating of metal-containing composites. Metal coordination is presented as a way to build stronger or more specific architectures, including ionic and functional-group crosslinks, nucleation and growth of MOF-like structures, and fibre-bridged assemblies relevant to devices such as perovskite solar cells.\n\nThe figure also highlights hydrophobic association and amphiphilic interactions, which help cellulose bind or exfoliate 2D nanomaterials and act as a biotemplate for crystal growth. Host–guest chemistry with macrocycles like cyclodextrins, cucurbiturils, crown ethers, and pillararenes adds selective molecular recognition. Finally, other weak interactions, including π–π stacking, cation–π attraction, and stereocomplexation, broaden the toolkit for designing cellulose-based supramolecular materials.",
